@page "/research"
@using Sorcery.ServerSide.Components.Footnotes

<SourceryTitle Title="Mat Gienieczko &ndash; Research"/>

<MudContainer MaxWidth="MaxWidth.Large">
    <MudPaper Height="100%" Width="100%" Class="pa-sm-16 pa-4 my-4">
        <FootnoteContainer>
        <article>
            <Header1>Research</Header1>
            <Paragraph>
                I am interested in things going <em><span class="fas fa-bolt"></span>fast<span class="fas fa-bolt"></span></em>,
                in particular databases. I believe there's tons of improvements we can bring to compilers, databases,
                operating systems, and other foundational technologies, to allow developers to achieve more with less.
            </Paragraph>
            <Paragraph>
                Of course, in @CurrentYear there is a revolution on the horizon, as an extremely important technological
                shift is poised to change everything &ndash; that's right, I'm of course talking about the advent
                of the post-Moore era! We need to build a truly robust database system that is able to leverage accelerators,
                non-homogenous memory configurations, and modern smart storage solutions.
            </Paragraph>
            <Paragraph>
                I am currently pursuing a PhD at the Technical University of Munich, under supervision by
                <LinkA Href="https://db.in.tum.de/people/sites/giceva/?lang=en">prof. Jana Giceva</LinkA>.
                If you're interested in our work or collaboration, please visit her site and/or take a look at
                <LinkA Href="https://db.in.tum.de/research/?lang=en">our group's research</LinkA>. Here I will highlight
                some of my work and papers.<Footnote>
                    Side note: anything that you read on this site is entirely my personal opinion and is in no way
                    endorsed by TUM or representative of our group's positions. There is a reason this personal site is
                    separate from my <LinkA Href="https://db.in.tum.de/people/sites/gienieczko/?lang=en">work db.in.tum.de site</LinkA>.
                </Footnote>
            </Paragraph>
            <section>
                <Header2>Projects</Header2>
                <Paragraph>
                    This is a list of projects I created or was heavily involved in &ndash; you can see all projects our
                    group is involved in <LinkA Href="https://db.in.tum.de/research/projects/?lang=en">here</LinkA>.
                
                    All of these are open-source and I am always happy to collaborate or to advise
                    student theses that work on improvements to them. 
                </Paragraph>
                <Header3>AnyBlox</Header3>
                <MudPaper MaxHeight="200px" MaxWidth="200px" Class="mx-auto mb-3 pa-3 sorcery-float-left d-none d-sm-flex" Elevation="0">
                    <MudImage Alt="AnyBlox logo" title="AnyBlox logo" Src="img/anyblox-logo.svg" Fluid="true" Height="200" Width="200" />
                </MudPaper>
                <MudPaper MaxHeight="200px" MaxWidth="200px" Class="mx-auto mb-3 pa-3 d-flex d-sm-none" Elevation="0">
                    <MudImage Alt="AnyBlox logo" title="AnyBlox logo" Src="img/anyblox-logo.svg" Fluid="true" Height="200" Width="200" />
                </MudPaper>
                <Paragraph>
                    <LinkA Href="@GitHub.AnyBlox">AnyBlox</LinkA> is <strong>a framework for reading arbitrary datasets using lightweight WebAssembly decoders bundled with the data</strong>.
                </Paragraph>
                <Paragraph>
                    We develop newer and better encodings and compression schemes for data, but your datasets are stuck on using whatever
                    your query engine supports reading &ndash; which, unless you're using the cutting edge, is not much.
                    We propose a different paradigm for data encodings &ndash; making datasets <em>decode themselves</em>.
                </Paragraph>
                <Paragraph>
                    WebAssembly decoders can be produced from basically any general-purpose language, which means you can
                    take your existing code, compile it to Wasm, and you're ready to use it with AnyBlox. Our framework
                    then allows you to seamlessly share memory with the decoder sandbox and thus makes your data portable,
                    secure and efficient to decode, and allows you to change the encoding without breaking consumers.
                </Paragraph>
                <Header3>rsonpath</Header3>
                <MudPaper MaxHeight="200px" MaxWidth="200px" Class="mx-auto mb-3 pa-3 sorcery-float-left d-none d-sm-flex" Elevation="0">
                    <MudImage Alt="rsonpath logo" title="rsonpath logo" Src="img/rsonquery-logo.svg" Fluid="true" Height="200" Width="200" />
                </MudPaper>
                <MudPaper MaxHeight="200px" MaxWidth="200px" Class="mx-auto mb-3 pa-3 d-flex d-sm-none" Elevation="0">
                    <MudImage Alt="rsonpath logo" title="rsonpath logo" Src="img/rsonquery-logo.svg" Fluid="true" Height="200" Width="200" />
                </MudPaper>
                <Paragraph>
                    The <LinkA Href="@GitHub.Rsonpath">rsonpath</LinkA> engine is 
                    <strong>the fastest JSONPath query engine</strong> with a tiny memory footprint.
                    Unlike other solutions, we never construct the JSON tree structure (DOM) in memory &ndash; the entire
                    algorithm is fully streaming.
                </Paragraph>
                <Paragraph>
                    We put emphasis on being a robust, production-ready solution for JSON processing that all but
                    guarantees your queries will be processed at GB/s throughput. The project currently lags behind
                    on implementing the full JSONPath spec because I'm putting more focus on my main PhD research,
                    which is why I'd be extremely happy with any contributions or students that wish to write a thesis
                    focusing on high-performance algorithms, SIMD, and automata.
                </Paragraph>
            </section>
            <section>
                <Header2>Publications</Header2>
                <MudTable Items="@Publications">
                    <HeaderContent>
                        <MudTh>Year</MudTh>
                        <MudTh>Title</MudTh>
                        <MudTh>Venue</MudTh>
                        <MudTh>Authors</MudTh>
                        <MudTh>Access</MudTh>
                        <MudTh>Talk</MudTh>
                    </HeaderContent>
                    <RowTemplate>
                        <MudTd DataLabel="Year">@context.Year</MudTd>
                        <MudTd DataLabel="Title"><strong>@context.Title</strong></MudTd>
                        <MudTd DataLabel="Venue">@context.Venue</MudTd>
                        <MudTd DataLabel="Authors"><em>@context.AuthorsString</em></MudTd>
                        <MudTd DataLabel="Access"><LinkA Href="@context.PdfLink">PDF</LinkA></MudTd>
                        <MudTd DataLabel="Talk">@if (context.HasTalk)
                                                {
                                                    <LinkA Href="@context.TalkLink"><span>Video</span></LinkA>
                                                }
                                                else
                                                {
                                                    <span>-</span>
                                                }
                        </MudTd>
                    </RowTemplate>
                </MudTable>
            </section>
        </article>
        </FootnoteContainer>
    </MudPaper>
</MudContainer>

@code {
    string CurrentYear => DateTime.Now.Year.ToString();

    public record class Publication(int Year, string Title, string Venue, string PdfLink)
    {
        private readonly string[] _authors = [];

        public string AuthorsString => string.Join(", ", _authors);

        public string? TalkLink { get; init; }

        public bool HasTalk => TalkLink is not null;
        
        public Publication(int year, string title, string venue, string pdfLink, IEnumerable<string> authors)
            : this(year, title, venue, pdfLink) => 
            _authors = authors.ToArray();
    }

    public List<Publication> Publications { get; } =
    [
        new (2025, "AnyBlox: A Framework for Self-Decoding Datasets", "VLDB '25 (Best Paper Award!)", "/anyblox-paper", [
            "Mateusz Gienieczko",
            "Maximilian Kuschewski",
            "Thomas Neumann",
            "Viktor Leis",
            "Jana Giceva"
        ]),
        new (2023, "Supporting Descendants in SIMD-Accelerated JSONPath", "ASPLOS '23", "rsonpath-paper", [
            "Mateusz Gienieczko",
            "Filip Murlak",
            "Charles Paperman"
        ])
        {
            TalkLink = "/rsonpath-asplos-talk"
        }
    ];

}
